.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_PENDING 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_pending ,
.Nm SSL_has_pending
.Nd check for readable bytes buffered in an SSL object
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_pending
.Fa "const SSL *ssl"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo SSL_has_pending
.Fa "const SSL *s"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Data is received in whole blocks known as records from the peer.
A whole record is processed (e.g.
decrypted) in one go and is buffered by OpenSSL until it is read by the
application via a call to
.Xr SSL_read_ex 3
or
.Xr SSL_read 3 .
.Pp
.Fn SSL_pending
returns the number of bytes which have been processed, buffered and are
available inside
.Fa ssl
for immediate read.
.Pp
If the
.Sy SSL
object's
.Em read_ahead
flag is set (see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_read_ahead 3 Ns ),
additional protocol bytes (beyond the current record) may have been read
containing more TLS/SSL records.
This also applies to DTLS and pipelining (see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_split_send_fragment 3 Ns ).
These additional bytes will be buffered by OpenSSL but will remain
unprocessed until they are needed.
As these bytes are still in an unprocessed state
.Fn SSL_pending
will ignore them.
Therefore, it is possible for no more bytes to be readable from the
underlying BIO (because OpenSSL has already read them) and for
.Fn SSL_pending
to return 0, even though readable application data bytes are available
(because the data is in unprocessed buffered records).
.Pp
.Fn SSL_has_pending
returns 1 if
.Fa s
has buffered data (whether processed or unprocessed) and 0 otherwise.
Note that it is possible for
.Fn SSL_has_pending
to return 1, and then a subsequent call to
.Xr SSL_read_ex 3
or
.Xr SSL_read 3
to return no data because the unprocessed buffered data when processed
yielded no application data (for example this can happen during
renegotiation).
It is also possible in this scenario for
.Fn SSL_has_pending
to continue to return 1 even after an
.Xr SSL_read_ex 3
or
.Xr SSL_read 3
call because the buffered and unprocessed data is not yet processable
(e.g.
because OpenSSL has only received a partial record so far).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn SSL_pending
returns the number of buffered and processed application data bytes that
are pending and are available for immediate read.
.Fn SSL_has_pending
returns 1 if there is buffered record data in the SSL object and 0
otherwise.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr SSL_read_ex 3 ,
.Xr SSL_read 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_read_ahead 3 ,
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_split_send_fragment 3 ,
.Xr ssl 7
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn SSL_has_pending
function was added in OpenSSL 1.1.0.
